<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Picenum</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Picenum was the ancient name of the region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apennines">Apennines</a> and the Adriatic, roughly the southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marche">Marche</a> and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abruzzo">Abruzzo</a>, home of the Picentes. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> conquered it in 268 BCE, and Augustus made it the fifth region of Italy. Its towns, such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ascoli-piceno">Asculum</a>, Firmum and Ancona, played a part in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/social-war-91-87-bce">Social War</a> of 91–88 BCE, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompey">Pompey</a>'s family drew its strength from the region.</p></div>
